everyone remembers the first rhythm game  weather was mimicking the smooth flow of  crap of the rapper stepping alongside  their favorite dance dance revelution  song or even smacking a pair of bongos  and donkey konga we all share those fond  memories of nailing that perfect string  of notes and with the crowd behind us  cheering us on whether it was virtual or  real life those games gave us all a  sense of accomplishment the genre  enjoyed a moderate amount of success  thanks in part to konami x' line of  arcade games but it wasn't until 2005  when guitar hero came along that rhythm  games really struck a chord with gamers  and began receiving worldwide attention  yes by the way that pun was intended and  while the big franchises like guitar  hero and rock band enjoyed the better  half of a decade as the best-selling  games in the world rhythm games start  falling victim to a similar problem with  the introduction of the guitar shaped  controllers it started becoming a race  to see who could emulate the rock star  experience the best it started with  guitars slowly expanded into full bands  then DJ turntables and before you knew  it we were up tore armpits and plastic  instruments sure this might not sound  too bad and might even be considered  beneficial that the genre was expanding  beyond this mostly Japanese player base  but the saturation of rhythm games  brought about a sense of obsolescence  with controllers piling up in people's  closets as each new iteration of a  series brought with it an updated  peripheral I mean admit it there's a  dusty plastic controller somewhere in  your house that's just begging to be  played with by 2010 almost five years at  the top of the gaming world rhythm games  finally started to falter sales  plummeted and they went from being the  hottest games on the planet - just  another expensive peripheral filled  gimmicky genre the studio's attempted to  win back fans by honing in on certain  demographics of music lovers but it was  still the same base game and they  weren't offering as many unique  experiences anymore it almost started to  feel like developers were losing sight  of what made rhythm games great in the  first place  but all of that might be changing it's  now 2016 and the state of VR gaming in  particular has led developers to try and  provide more immersive experiences to  the public and to of the releases for  the PlayStation VR just so happen to be  rythm games there's thumper a game where  you play a space beetle speeding down a  track hitting jumps and curves to the  beat and then there's res infinite a  remake of the original 2001 psychedelic  shooter but which also includes a brand  new area designed specifically for VR  now gameplay wise these two couldn't  play more differently thumper is a  pulse-pounding rhythm violence game as  they call it requiring lightning quick  reactions to succeed while res infinite  is more of a relaxing experience letting  you create your own beats and allowing  the music to just wash over you  but if there's one thing that they do  share aside from the trapeze space like  settings it's their ability to entrace  the player in the music and the visuals  even though they can both be played on a  normal television they are two games  whose existence is amplified by the use  of virtual reality with the VR headset  on and with sensory immersion in full  effect  you are immediately pulled into the  game's world bobbing your head to the  beat moving your body in tune with the  music they may be the only game so far  where I felt the VR technology to be the  engrossing experience it's promised to  be yes the prospect of VR being the new  guiding light for rhythm games might be  hard to stomach considering the platform  is still at this point in its infancy  but it's also exciting to dream about  all the new games that we can immerse  ourselves in and all the new ways that  we can achieve that same feeling that we  had when we first picked up a guitar  hero controller and this is why I feel  like VR could be the thing to resurrect  the rhythm game genre on their own  rhythm games can be tense what Phil  d'affaires  but the pairing of VR simply adds to  that visceral satisfying high that we  all crave while playing these games the  critical success of both res Infinite  and thumper prove that can be done well  but more importantly they've proven that  you don't need to be playing an  instrument or even getting down on the  dance floor to be absorbed by the music  what they've done is essentially widen  the playing field with what's possible  in VR and rhythm games and honestly I  can't wait to see what the future holds  for both  what do you think about the marriage  between rhythm games and VR which series  would you like to see most on the  platform  I'm personally holding out for a new  parappa but let me know in the comments  what you think and thanks for watching
 

